WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.549 --> 00:00:05.850
Though it was something of a box office disappointment
when it first hit theaters in 1997, Luc Besson's

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:11.000
whacked-out sci-fi epic The FIfth Element
has since become a cult classic thanks to

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:16.400
gonzo visuals and quirky performances from
stars Bruce Willis, Milla Jovovich, and Chris

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:17.400
Tucker.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.859
But despite the film's popularity, there has
never been a follow-up.

00:00:19.859 --> 00:00:23.470
Here's a look at some of the reasons why The
Fifth Element never got a sequel.

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:25.749
It wasn't a huge hit

00:00:25.749 --> 00:00:29.600
The Fifth Element didn't fare particularly
well during its theatrical release in the

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.859
United States, which was a problem because
at the time, the movie's $90 million-plus

00:00:34.859 --> 00:00:39.829
price tag made it the most expensive film
ever produced outside of Hollywood.

00:00:39.829 --> 00:00:45.260
While the film would go on to break some international
box office records by pulling in $263 million

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:50.859
worldwide, it scored just $63 million of that
take from U.S. theaters - which is probably

00:00:50.859 --> 00:00:55.410
a big reason an equally expensive sequel wasn't
quick to get the green light.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:56.760
The story was complete

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.690
Though Besson originally envisioned The Fifth
Element as part of a trilogy, the story of

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:05.190
main characters Korben and Leeloo actually
feels pretty complete at the end of the film.

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:09.140
He's rediscovered his vigor for life, she's
found a reason to want to save the Earth,

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:12.840
the pair have found love together, and in
the process, they've literally brought peace

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:13.840
to the galaxy.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.830
Sure, Besson could probably find a clever
way to shake these two out of their well-earned

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:18.830
happiness.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:22.670
At this point, though, it might feel more
like a desperate cash grab, especially considering

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:24.590
Willis is now in his mid-60's.

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:28.890
Much as fans would like to see more, nobody
wants to see The Fifth Element's legacy tarnished

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:31.210
by an unnecessary second act.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:34.650
"It's a very nice hat."

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:35.740
"You liked it?!"

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:39.700
Offscreen drama

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:44.420
One of the biggest complicating factors when
it comes to a sequel is the offscreen drama

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:46.390
between Besson and Jovovich.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:50.090
During filming of The Fifth Element, the two
became romantically involved and got married

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:52.080
not long after the film's premiere.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.090
It didn't last, though, as their relationship
fell apart on the set of their 1999 collaboration

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.350
The Messenger: The Story of Joan of Arc.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:02.090
The film flopped, and the couple divorced
the same year.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:06.340
Still, anything is possible — according
to Jovovich, their relationship worked a lot

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.160
better on set than off set.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.580
She told the Scottish Daily Record,

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:14.100
"If we could make movies 365 days a year,
we would still be together.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.280
Luc and I have this unique creative connection.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:17.320
We did get married.

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.280
But we're not together anymore in that sort
of particular context.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.700
We're best friends, apart from being together
or not.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:24.860
He loves me, I love him."

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:26.890
Besson's enigmatic career

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:30.210
When it comes to sequels, you should always
strike while the iron is hot.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:34.000
But after the mess that was The Messenger:
The Story of Joan of Arc and the dissolution

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.069
of his marriage to Jovovich, Besson went the
other direction, stepping away from directing

00:02:38.069 --> 00:02:41.569
entirely for six years to focus on writing
instead.

00:02:41.569 --> 00:02:45.430
That break may have squandered any momentum
for a Fifth Element sequel, but that's par

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:49.010
for the course for Besson, who doesn't seem
to be interested in revisiting his past as

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:50.120
a director.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:54.360
Other than a couple of animated films, Besson
has never directed a sequel, even passing

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:58.130
the reins of the proposed follow-up to his
2014 hit Lucy.

00:02:58.130 --> 00:03:01.610
It seems it would have to take something pretty
special to convince him to return to the world

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:03.500
of The Fifth Element.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:04.860
Special effect frustrations

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:09.520
When The Fifth Element arrived in theaters,
it was regarded as bona fide special effects

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:10.520
bonanza.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:14.100
But according to Besson, the special effects
in The Fifth Element were frustrating to work

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:17.880
with, because the technology didn't exist
yet to truly fulfil his vision.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:21.070
Perhaps if the effects had existed at the
time, Besson would have moved forward with

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:24.819
the rest of the trilogy, but now that the
technology does finally exist, he seems to

00:03:24.819 --> 00:03:26.550
have moved on to other projects.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:27.570
Speaking of which...

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:29.220
A spiritual sequel

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:33.060
Though Besson has suggested in interviews
that he might be open to filming other stories

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:37.380
set in the same universe as The Fifth Element,
for the last decade most of his energy seems

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:41.300
to have been turned towards his big screen
adaptation of the long running French comic

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:44.590
book series Valerian and the City of a Thousand
Planets.

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:48.950
Filled with mind-blowing imagery only made
possible by advances in effects over the past

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:53.490
20 years, Valerian and the City of a Thousand
Planets unfortunately failed to launch at

00:03:53.490 --> 00:04:01.070
the box office, taking in just $225 million
worldwide against a huge $177 million budget.

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:05.270
Since the film underperformed, the future
of the franchise is up in the air, but considering

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:09.489
Besson was reportedly planning to break his
own rule against sequels by turning Valerian

00:04:09.489 --> 00:04:14.099
into a trilogy, it seems as though his heart
just isn't in The Fifth Element any more.

00:04:14.099 --> 00:04:15.099
Never say never

00:04:15.099 --> 00:04:18.579
Though there's an argument to be made that
it's often better to leave your fanbase wanting

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:22.389
more, Besson still shows a lot of affection
for the world of The Fifth Element, and that

00:04:22.389 --> 00:04:26.080
would seem to leave the door open—if only
slightly—for a sequel.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.770
Even if Korben and Leeloo aren't at the center
of the story, we'd still love to see where

00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:34.070
Besson might take things next—just as long
as it isn't a Ruby Rhod origin story.

00:04:34.070 --> 00:04:38.110
All due respect to Besson and Chris Tucker,
but some characters are just more fun in small

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:39.139
doses.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:41.300
"All night looong!

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:41.900
All night.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:44.800
Ooooh!"

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.040
Thanks for watching!

